A||N||7||pherder||For you who do their own brakes ... WARNING!!!||||4||
Z||000000||pherder||09-09-2000||06:10 PM||||Please see the following thread:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/003584.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/003584.html</A> <P>Thought I would post this to the Tech Forum in case it is missed on the General Fiero Chat Forum.<P>Paul||||reg||4
Z||000001||Gokart||09-09-2000||07:19 PM||||Good info! Sorry that happened to you but thanks for sharing. Would you be able to get the bolts off later though if you put locktight on the threads?||||reg||11
Z||000002||pherder||09-09-2000||07:42 PM||||I am now using Locktight's "blue" formula since it is the kind that will allow disassembly.  The "red" formula needs 500 degrees F to be able to disassemble the parts.  I know that the brakes get hotter then that but I didn't think that the mounting bolts to the uprights would get that hot.  Plus I didn't want them to become one with the uprights!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>BTW, the factory puts "something" on the bolts since it was on them when I took them off.<P>Paul<BR><p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 09-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gokart||09-09-2000||08:14 PM||||So are you recommending the "blue" or the "red locktite? Blue would be good for future removal but if the reds no good above 500 and the breaks get hotter than that.....||||reg||5
Z||000004||pherder||09-09-2000||09:11 PM||||Well, I don't know if I am recommending the "blue" but I am using it.<P>The point of the "red" is to make the connection solid where it will NOT come apart UNLESS you supply 500 degree F to the parts.  I guess that destroys the material and you can take them apart.<P>While at the local auto supply house, those were my two choices and I went "blue" deciding that I might want to take them apart some day in the future.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Paul<P>||||reg||12
Z||000005||mrfiero||09-09-2000||09:26 PM||||Blue is the recommended type.  If you buy a brand new GM caliper bolt it comes with the blue lock tite already on it......for about $10 a bolt it should!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000006||lawrence||09-10-2000||10:40 AM||||I have the Helms book for my 88.  It doesn't say anything about using thread locking goop.  However, it DOES give torque specs for tightening the caliper to the knuckle.  If you do that, I wouldn't think they would fall off.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A> - WebElectric Magazine<p>[This message has been edited by lawrence (edited 09-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||09-10-2000||11:31 AM||||A friend's 88 Reatta had the caliper bolts break on the right front. It destroyed the caliper and damaged the wheel. The caliper got wedged between the strut and wheel and locked up the right front. Luckily, he had just exited the interstate and was only about a block from the shop. Always replace the caliper bolts whenever servicing the brakes!||||reg||1
